WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.460
Last time, juice pressed from yeast fermented sugar, and controls showed no living

2
00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:10.500
cell was needed. So if not life, what in that flask was doing the work?

3
00:00:13.933 --> 00:00:16.333
Buchner's answer lay in the juice itself.

4
00:00:17.693 --> 00:00:22.733
The carrier of the activity, he wrote, must be a dissolved substance, undoubtedly a

5
00:00:22.733 --> 00:00:25.593
protein. He named it zymase.

6
00:00:26.813 --> 00:00:32.833
His 1907 Nobel Prize is often called the first for a fundamentally biochemical

7
00:00:32.833 --> 00:00:33.833
discovery.

8
00:00:34.767 --> 00:00:36.367
His worker had relatives.

9
00:00:37.707 --> 00:00:42.227
Some ferments, such as pepsin and the invertase of yeast, already worked outside

10
00:00:42.227 --> 00:00:49.527
cells. In 1877, Wilhelm Kühne named them enzymes, from the Greek for "in leaven".

11
00:00:50.567 --> 00:00:56.027
Kühne studied digestion and vision, and believed both were chemistry waiting to be

12
00:00:56.027 --> 00:00:57.027
lifted out of the cell.

13
00:00:58.287 --> 00:01:01.667
His word named the chemical worker, not the cell or the tissue.

14
00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:04.410
Workers are half the story.

15
00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:07.030
The other half is the rules.

16
00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:15.010
In 1854, Marcellin Berthelot joined glycerol to fatty acids and remade natural fats,

17
00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:19.710
releasing water. Add water, and the reaction runs back.

18
00:01:20.733 --> 00:01:26.333
That back-and-forth is equilibrium chemistry, the same kind that governs reactions

19
00:01:26.333 --> 00:01:27.413
in the mineral world.

20
00:01:28.613 --> 00:01:31.073
The body's molecules needed no special law.

21
00:01:32.493 --> 00:01:35.213
Berthelot also made new fats that nature does not.

22
00:01:36.267 --> 00:01:38.007
So here is the one idea.

23
00:01:39.167 --> 00:01:43.167
Where vitalists placed a force, chemists found ordinary chemistry.

24
00:01:44.507 --> 00:01:46.647
Its catalysts can be lifted out of the cell.

25
00:01:47.887 --> 00:01:50.307
And they follow the same rules as everything else.

26
00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:53.430
Buchner gave his worker one name.

27
00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:59.950
Predict: when chemists later took zymase apart, did they find a single substance, or

28
00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:00.950
several?

29
00:02:06.417 --> 00:02:13.277
Several. Harden and Young split zymase into enzymes plus small, heat-stable

30
00:02:13.277 --> 00:02:17.817
cofactors. Chief among them was cozymase, now known as NAD.

31
00:02:19.317 --> 00:02:22.877
Together they run glycolysis, plus two steps that make ethanol.

32
00:02:24.257 --> 00:02:29.037
A whole process had become a set of reactions, open to study one at a time.

33
00:02:30.067 --> 00:02:34.767
The catalysts Kühne named and Buchner freed are, nearly all of them, proteins.

34
00:02:36.267 --> 00:02:40.527
Even Wöhler's urea, made in a flask with no kidney, has its own team.

35
00:02:41.887 --> 00:02:46.667
Your liver builds it right now with five enzymes, working in a loop called the urea

36
00:02:46.667 --> 00:02:47.667
cycle.

37
00:02:48.117 --> 00:02:52.237
In the chemistry of the cell, the vital force had nowhere left to hide.

38
00:02:53.557 --> 00:02:58.637
In its place: workers you can pull out and study, obeying the same rules that govern

39
00:02:58.637 --> 00:02:59.677
rocks and salts.
